If your program uses too much memory, or forgets to clean up memory it no longer needs, it can lead toslow performanceor evencrashes.

That's why understanding memory is important. It gives you more control and helps avoid common bugs.

In C++, you can usepointersto access and change memory directly.

This is powerful, but also risky. If you use a pointer the wrong way, you could accidentally change or damage other parts of your program's memory.

In the next chapter, you'll learn how to create memory manually using pointers - with thenewkeyword, and how to free it usingdelete.

